<TextPage
  title="Privacy"
  eyebrow="Privacy notice"
  heading="What we keep, and why"
  lede="AURA keeps as little personal data as it can. This page explains what it holds about readers and about the people its records name, under Nigeria’s Data Protection Act 2023."
  description="How AURA handles personal data: Morning Brief subscribers, people named in records, and disputes."
  updated="2026-11-07"
>
  <h2>If you subscribe to the Morning Brief</h2>
  <p>We store:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>your email address;</li>
    <li>the choices you made: which kinds of news, which region, how often, and whether delivery is paused;</li>
    <li>when you signed up, confirmed, or unsubscribed;</li>
    <li>a random code that makes your confirm, change-topics and unsubscribe links work.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We use these only to send you the brief you asked for. We do not sell or share them, and we do not use
    them for advertising. The addresses are kept in a database run by Cloudflare, and the emails are sent
    through an email delivery service. Neither may use your address for anything else.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>How long:</strong> a sign-up that is never confirmed is deleted after 30 days. When you
    unsubscribe we stop sending at once, and keep the address and dates only as long as we need them to
    show that you left and to make sure you are not emailed again.
  </p>

  <h2>If you send a dispute</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href={href("dispute/")}>dispute form</a> stores your name, email address, your relationship to the
    record and your message, so we can look into it and reply. It is never published. We keep it as long as
    we need to handle the dispute and to show what we did about it. We do not store your IP address: a
    one-way code made from it limits how many disputes one sender can send in a day.
  </p>

  <h2>People named in our records</h2>
  <p>
    AURA’s records name company owners, directors and officials because news outlets reported on them. We
    publish them in the public interest, as a summary of journalism about who controls Nigerian companies.
    Each record links to the article it came from. Records are drawn from news reports by software; they are
    not the official CAC register and are not checked against it.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a record names you and it is wrong, or you object to it, use the <a href={href("dispute/")}>dispute form</a>.
    We will review it, reply, and correct or remove the record where that is right. Changes are listed on the
    <a href={href("corrections/")}>corrections page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask to see the personal data we hold about you, to have it corrected, or to have it deleted.
    Every brief email has links to change your choices or unsubscribe. For anything else, send a request
    through the <a href={href("dispute/?ref=privacy%20request")}>contact form</a> (choose “Other”), or reply to
    any brief email. We answer within 30 days. If you are not satisfied, you can complain to the Nigeria Data
    Protection Commission.
  </p>

  <h2>Browsing the site</h2>
  <p>
    The site sets no advertising or tracking cookies. Your browser remembers your light or dark theme and
    which alerts you have read; that stays on your device. Like any website, our host receives your IP
    address to deliver the page.
  </p>
</TextPage>
